/** @page cosmology_quijote Quijote exact-initial-condition comparison

@section quijote_scope Scope and qualification
The first target is the standard massless-neutrino Quijote fiducial realization:
512^3 equal-weight collisionless particles in a 1000 Mpc/h periodic box, starting
at z=127. Published 2LPT positions and velocities are imported exactly to their
stored precision; an internal 2LPT generator is not needed. Current local tests
are small-fixture checks, not evidence that this production run has succeeded.
Parallel HDF5 is deferred until after a successful 512^3 run. The offline serial converter accepts original Gadget format-1 files and the
current losslessly compressed Gadget HDF5 initial conditions. HDF5 decoding
requires h5py and, for the official Blosc release, hdf5plugin. This is an input
conversion step; simulation input remains the canonical serial-read binary.
Input signatures select the reader; mixed formats, nonlocal HDF5 storage,
unsupported filters and declared nonzero truncation bits are rejected.

@section quijote_units Background, units and density
Raw Gadget coordinates are kpc/h and raw velocities obey
@f$u=v_{\rm pec}/\sqrt a@f$. The canonical import is
@f[x=x_{\rm Gadget}/1000,\qquad p=a^{3/2}u/100=a v_{\rm pec}/100.@f]
Apply the square-root factor once, not again after a reader has already returned
physical peculiar velocities. The type-1 masses must be positive and equal;
their physical value in Msun/h is retained separately from unit PM weights.
For N_p particles and N_c force cells, density contrast is
@f$\delta_i=N_c M_i/N_p-1@f$, where M_i is the deposited unit-weight mass.
The diagnostic density RMS is @f$[\sum_i\delta_i^2/N_c]^{1/2}@f$,
independent of the ratio of particle and mesh counts. The background is the
existing flat radiation-free matter-plus-Lambda model. Header epoch, box,
Omega_m, Omega_Lambda and h must match configuration at relative 1e-10; this
checks round-trip metadata, not a tolerance on physical evolution. No input
power-spectrum normalization or linear-growth rescaling is applied.

@section quijote_wire Binary contract
The portable stream is little-endian IEEE float64 with a 128-byte header.
Python's struct descriptor is `<8sQQ6dQ48x`: magic `IPPLPS01`, file-local and
global uint64 counts, six doubles (a, L, Omega_m, Omega_Lambda, h, physical
particle mass), a uint64 flags field, then 48 reserved zero bytes.
Records are `<Q6d`: zero-based uint64 ID, x/y/z, p_x/p_y/p_z (56 bytes).
A converted complete input has flag 1 and record j must contain ID j. Snapshot
shards have flag 0, may be empty, and carry the same global count and epoch.
The exact file length is 128+56*Nlocal; truncation, trailing data, unsupported
flags, duplicate/missing/out-of-order input IDs and nonfinite values fail.
Input x=L wraps to zero with no artificial edge displacement. Imported displacement
RMS measures motion since import, not displacement from an undisplaced lattice. Source Gadget
IDs are explicitly converted from one-based to zero-based. The converter
uses bounded chunks and disk-backed ordering, and publishes checksums and
source metadata in a JSON sidecar. HDF5 provenance includes dataset dtypes,
storage chunks, filter descriptions and the official CompressionInfo metadata.
Decoded HDF5 chunks are bounded to 64 MiB; particle iteration remains bounded
by the requested chunk size. The sidecar is retained by the benchmark runner; matching headers alone do not
establish catalogue identity, LPT order, baryon fraction or initial spectrum. The
runner records those provenance limits explicitly; C++ also validates the binary contract.

@section quijote_execution Execution and data movement
Rank zero reads bounded batches and distributes them through a dedicated MPI
communicator. Each rank fills its local host mirrors and copies x,p,IDs and
initial reference positions once into Kokkos execution memory. Migration,
CIC deposition, FFT force and KDK remain distributed in the configured space.
Fatal input/I/O errors abort while collective simulation objects are still alive,
avoiding destructor collectives while peers await data.
Snapshots explicitly mirror local particle data and publish per-rank binary
files before rank zero publishes a manifest row. No full global particle array
or ID sort is needed by this production path. Global counts are exact; two
modulo-2^64 ID signatures and range checks detect migration corruption but do
not constitute a collision-free uniqueness proof. Initial ordered-ID checking
is exact. Floating-point sums may vary with rank/thread execution order;
regressions use declared tolerances, not bitwise evolution identity.
On the Merlin launcher, @f$N_{\rm ranks}N_{\rm host\ threads}\leq4@f$ keeps
MPI2/OpenMP2 validation within the same four-CPU allocation as MPI4/OpenMP1
production. Device binding and numerical kernels are unchanged.
The initial serial reader can bottleneck startup and snapshot mirrors consume
additional host memory; both must be measured at 512^3 before changing I/O.

@section quijote_epochs Epochs and timestep refinement
The existing @f$\ln a@f$ base schedule is augmented with requested
@f$a=1/(1+z)@f$ endpoints. An output splits an interval and uses the same
production KDK equations; no interpolated or relabelled state is written.
Near-identical endpoints within eight relative machine epsilons are replaced
by the requested epoch to avoid a vanishing interval. The final endpoint is now
exactly aFinal even with no requested outputs; compared with the previous
exponential evaluation this can change final-step rounding by a few ulps. Distinct requested epochs
within sixteen relative machine epsilons are rejected. Output coordinates and
momenta are synchronized after the second kick. `nt` is the number of base
intervals; metadata records the actual number after output splits. The final
epoch appears once even when explicitly requested. Compare refinement pairs
with the same output list. Adding endpoints changes finite-step trajectories;
therefore temporal convergence must be checked again for the chosen schedule.

@section quijote_observables Accuracy and reference limits
The measurement mesh is independent of the force mesh. Analyze IPPL and the
reference using the same periodic deposition, Fourier normalization
@f$P(k)=L^3\langle|\delta_k|^2\rangle@f$, window convention and shell bins.
Default raw particle power avoids assuming an evolved lattice is Poisson;
any optional shot-noise subtraction is identified explicitly. Small direct
Fourier sums provide an independent estimator check. A power ratio and phase
cross-correlation diagnose distinct errors. Quijote's evolved data may include
lossy coordinate/velocity quantization, whereas its published ICs do not;
record that precision floor rather than claiming particlewise machine accuracy.
The initial HDF5 adapter accepts lossless input only; a future measurement reader
for lossy evolved HDF5 snapshots must explicitly retain their quantization floor.
Mesh/time controls and independent realizations are required before reporting
a resolved k range. The proposed percent-level target is not automatically
met because execution and file-integrity checks pass.

@section quijote_catalogue Catalogue provenance and access
The standard realization is the directory /Snapshots/fiducial/0/ICs/ in the
Rusty Globus collection e0eae0aa-5bca-11ea-9683-0e56c063f437. A matching header
alone does not distinguish fiducial from fiducial_ZA. Preserve the authenticated
transfer receipt and exact source hashes together with the converter manifest.
Official references: https://quijote-simulations.readthedocs.io/en/latest/access.html
and https://github.com/lgarrison/quijote-compression/blob/main/compress_gadget.py.
The user's current goal authorizes the actual 512 cubed A100 run. A fresh source
snapshot and CUDA build/validation precede a full-count import/short-evolution
pilot, then the requested z=1,0.5,0 evolution. Small synthetic validation cannot
substitute for the published realization or establish its full-size completion.

@section quijote_next Subsequent production work
Serial conversion, import and per-rank binary snapshots are the initial path.
Parallel HDF5 is a later step explicitly conditional on a successful 512^3 run.
The comparison tool also supports a chosen redshift-space line of sight and raw
quadrupole/hexadecapole estimators, with
@f$s_\parallel=x_\parallel+p_\parallel/(a^2 E(a))@f$; E(a)=H(a)/H0.
Small analytical tests verify the velocity conversion and multipole weights;
cosmological accuracy still requires comparison to the reference and refinement.
Qualified production restart remains subsequent work. Do not infer restart
capability from the word checkpoint on diagnostic CSVs.
*/
